Given the function:
f(x) = \sum_{k=1}^{10} kx^k
Expanding the sum, we get:
f(x) = x + 2x^2 + 3x^3 + \dots + 9x^9 + 10x^{10} \quad \dots(i)
Multiply equation (i) by x:
xf(x) = x^2 + 2x^3 + \dots + 9x^{10} + 10x^{11} \quad \dots(ii)
Subtract equation (ii) from equation (i):
f(x) - xf(x) = (x + 2x^2 + \dots + 10x^{10}) - (x^2 + 2x^3 + \dots + 10x^{11})
f(x)(1-x) = x + x^2 + x^3 + \dots + x^{10} - 10x^{11}
The sum \(x + x^2 + \dots + x^{10}\) is a geometric series with first term \(x\), common ratio \(x\), and 10 terms. Its sum is \(\frac{x(1-x^{10})}{1-x}\).
f(x)(1-x) = \frac{x(1-x^{10})}{1-x} - 10x^{11}
Rearranging to solve for \(f(x)\):
f(x) = \frac{x(1-x^{10})}{(1-x)^2} - \frac{10x^{11}}{1-x}
To find \(f'(x)\), we differentiate the expression \((1-x)^2 f(x) = x(1-x^{10}) - 10x^{11}(1-x)\) with respect to x:
\frac{d}{dx} \left[ (1-x)^2 f(x) \right] = \frac{d}{dx} \left[ x(1-x^{10}) - 10x^{11}(1-x) \right]
(1-x)^2 f'(x) + f(x) \cdot 2(1-x)(-1) = \left[ (1-x^{10}) + x(-10x^9) \right] - \left[ 110x^{10}(1-x) + 10x^{11}(-1) \right]
(1-x)^2 f'(x) - 2(1-x)f(x) = (1-x^{10} - 10x^{10}) - (110x^{10} - 110x^{11} - 10x^{11})
(1-x)^2 f'(x) - 2(1-x)f(x) = 1 - 11x^{10} - 110x^{10} + 120x^{11}
(1-x)^2 f'(x) - 2(1-x)f(x) = 1 - 121x^{10} + 120x^{11}
Now, substitute \(x=2\) into this equation:
(1-2)^2 f'(2) - 2(1-2)f(2) = 1 - 121(2)^{10} + 120(2)^{11}
(-1)^2 f'(2) - 2(-1)f(2) = 1 - 121(2)^{10} + 120(2)^{11}
f'(2) + 2f(2) = 1 - 121(2)^{10} + 120 \cdot 2 \cdot 2^{10}
f'(2) + 2f(2) = 1 - 121(2)^{10} + 240(2)^{10}
f'(2) + 2f(2) = 1 + (240 - 121)(2)^{10}
f'(2) + 2f(2) = 1 + 119(2)^{10}
The problem statement is \(2f(2) - f'(2) = 119(2)^n + 1\). However, our derivation yields \(2f(2) + f'(2) = 119(2)^{10} + 1\). Assuming the problem intended \(2f(2) + f'(2)\) or there is a sign convention difference in the problem statement, we compare our result with the given form:
119(2)^{10} + 1 = 119(2)^n + 1
Comparing the exponents, we find:
n = 10

